WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.770
JASON WESTON: Now, I
don't know about you,

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:06.720
but these days I'm using AI in
my everyday life quite a lot,

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.980
like to help me create
art, write code, write

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.400
formal letters, ask questions,
brainstorm all kinds of stuff,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.890
and that's only really been
possible very recently.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:21.470
I think AI research
is we're pretty

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:25.070
privileged to be in this
time in human history,

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:29.990
and see these improvements
happening in real time.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:32.670
And we've seen how
quickly they evolve,

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:36.500
but I still think
there's a lot left to do.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.480
I think we can expect still
more large improvements even

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.640
in the near future
and next few years,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.480
and we've seen that even in
the last months with O1, R1,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.550
and so on.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:58.160
Big improvements in various
benchmarks in reasoning.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.760
And after all that, it's a
very young field overall,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.950
but like that subfield with
learning chain of thought.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.190
I mean, chain of thought itself
is only really from 2022,

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:15.080
so, yeah, I think
it's really impressive

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.360
how fast things are moving.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.260
So in the context
of this talk, I'm

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.690
going to be talking
about methods

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:28.080
for self-improving
language models,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.250
and that means getting
better at tasks in particular

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.180
that will mean that they're
getting better at reasoning so

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.390
that they can better get
better at those tasks.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:43.530
So for an AI to
train itself, I mean,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.760
that means it needs to do this
introspection as its training,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.970
so normal, classical training
was like this fixed data set

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.430
and it would learn
from it, but that's

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.470
kind of moving now a little
bit where in like reinforcement

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.460
learning setups, and
particularly with reinforcement

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.270
learning from AI feedback models
are sort of training themselves

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.110
in a much more
sophisticated way.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.840
For example, we
can imagine models

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.050
that create new challenges,
tasks to train themselves

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.780
on, they can evaluate themselves
whether they're getting them

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.560
right, so we call
that self-rewarding,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.500
and then update themselves based
on what they've understood,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.190
so that means then they're going
to be getting more knowledge,

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.660
more reasoning ability.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.380
And so a research
question is, can they

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:34.730
do this kind of
training on themselves,

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.730
and can that help them
become superhuman?

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.810
So I have a picture down here
of one particular approach

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.240
for that that's called
self-rewarding language

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.550
models, which I'm going to
be talking about a bit later.

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.100
But in terms of
reasoning, when we're

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.360
doing this kind
of self-improving,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:59.440
you can look at this as two
different ways of improving

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.000
reasoning, so these are
called system 1 and system 2

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.750
sometimes by people.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:11.800
So system 1, like in humans,
we see that as reactive

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.790
and relies on associations,
and in machine learning models,

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.170
or LLMs it can be
the same thing.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:22.705
So you can think of
the transformer itself,

00:03:22.705 --> 00:03:26.860
the neural network, as this
kind of performing this system 1

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.323
reasoning, so where you
have this hidden states

00:03:31.323 --> 00:03:32.740
in the neural
network that's going

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.320
through a whole bunch
of multiplications,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:41.830
attentions, and so on, that it
can be seen as your system 1

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:42.530
reasoning.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:45.350
So in that case, you have
a fixed compute per token,

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.950
you're directly
outputting the answer,

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.920
but there's some
problems with LLMs

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.510
as we have them
now, in particular,

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.870
they can learn spurious
unwanted correlations,

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.020
there's hallucination,
sycophancy, jailbreaking,

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.800
and I'm going to show you
some of those things later.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.110
And then you can try
and fix this stuff

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.240
with what's called
system 2, so that's

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.680
more deliberate and
effortful thinking,

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.770
and the most common way we
think of that system 2 today

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:19.700
is by generating language tokens
that are a chain of thought.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.765
So now, that's not just
the pushing these numbers

00:04:23.765 --> 00:04:25.390
through the neural
net, you're actually

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:29.800
looking at the tokens, the
neural net outputs as a thought

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:35.000
stream before the final tokens
that will be the final response.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.400
Now what's powerful
when you do that is

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.040
that system 2 chain
of thought reasoning

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.610
can actually do a
whole bunch of stuff

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:49.850
like planning,
search, verification,

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:54.220
various kinds of reasoning,
even though it's generating

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:59.290
autoregressively left to right,
it could still potentially do

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:04.160
all that sets of
thoughts, as you'll see,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.425
models like Deep Sea 103 can do.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:12.430
Yeah, so those are the
two kinds of things

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:15.190
that we can effectively
improve in, try and improve

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.830
like the architecture or
the weights of the model,

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:20.110
and improve system
one, or try and improve

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.390
how it generates these
chains of thought,

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:26.450
or other ways of doing system
2 before it outputs a response.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.000
I mean, obviously
they're related

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.780
because it's the same language
model doing both things.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.840
That's the goal, try and
do this self-learning

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.160
to improve these things.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:45.190
First, I'm going to go through
some history of how we got where

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:50.920
we are today, and then talk
about more recent works

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:54.200
from the last year or so after
I go through this history.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:57.790
But before we're going to start
pre-2020, so prehistory if you

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:03.710
like, so language modeling
itself that goes way back.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:11.140
I mean, yeah, Claude Shannon
in 1950s talked about that,

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:16.660
so it's about taking language
and predicting the next token

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:19.910
or the next word at each
step, and obviously,

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:23.680
that's the pre-training
mechanism we use for large

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.670
language models, and that's
how they get their name,

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.170
where you're just trying to
predict the probability of these

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:29.720
tokens.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.680
We do this now on
these massive corpora

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.426
and pick up those
probability distributions.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:45.730
So, in 2003, there's
one of the first methods

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:49.830
for doing that with a
neural network that's

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:54.270
Ben Duchamp and Vincent,
so they had word

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:59.490
embeddings at the first layer
and then various layers, tanh,

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:03.720
and softmax to get
the final prediction.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:10.380
And I just put an interesting
quote there in their conclusions

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:13.770
that they say there's probably
a lot more to be done to improve

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.102
the model, the architecture,
computational efficiency,

00:07:17.102 --> 00:07:19.560
and they'd like to increase
the capacity without increasing

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.290
training time because they want
to deal with corpora of hundreds

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.810
and millions of words,
so that's kind of where

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:29.310
we got to some years later.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:31.270
Now, that was 2003.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:35.370
Most of the 2000s actually, the
research wasn't really focused

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.280
on neural networks.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:38.800
It was focused on
support vector machines,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.110
so these kernel classifiers.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:45.100
I worked in that area
too, as you can see,

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:51.750
there's a paper from me, and
it was kind of that, well,

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:55.810
held back the LLM story,
at least for a few years.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:59.470
So here I have a
slide for myself

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:03.120
and Ronald Collobert
from 2008 presented

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.703
ICML, where we were trying to
argue that the support vector

00:08:07.703 --> 00:08:09.120
machines of the
day that were used

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.170
in these Cascades of systems,
parts of speech tagging

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:15.495
and named entity
recognizers and parses,

00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:18.120
and it's not a good idea because
we could train the whole thing

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.420
end to end with
a neural network,

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:25.590
and no one was really
doing that then.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:30.930
So we had a paper at that
time, a unified architecture

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:36.750
for NLP, which we considered
a successor to the Bengio work

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.510
from 2003 there.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:46.590
So here what we did was we also
had a neural network with word

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:51.240
embeddings at the first layer,
then convolutional layers a max

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.180
over time, which is a
kind of proto attention,

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.560
and followed by a softmax.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:02.550
And we show that you could train
this model as a masked language

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:06.240
model on something like
Wikipedia, which actually

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.150
was pretty tricky at the time
with the compute we had, and it

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.560
could learn some pretty
interesting things,

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.290
like you could see from
the word similarities.

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:16.500
This is obviously
pre word2vec, which

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.770
I think was 2013, which
is a simpler model.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:24.910
This is a more complex neural
net that you could do that,

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:26.770
so you could pre-train
on Wikipedia,

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:30.750
and then afterwards you could
fine tune on tasks of interest

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:34.500
like part of speech tagging,
named entity, chunking, S.R.L,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.750
and that worked.

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:45.690
So yeah, this was a predecessor
to where we are today as well.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:50.250
It was interesting because at
the time that wasn't appreciated

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.690
by everyone in
the NLP community,

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.060
so in the Stanford
natural language group,

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:58.410
they had a reading group
there where they called it

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:05.010
a bullshit ICML paper that was
kind of on their public website.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:11.220
And then this same paper 1 Test
of Time Award at ICML 2018,

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:16.080
and then Stanford
University then

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.480
opened the Center for
Research on foundation models

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:22.740
and their position paper
called that same paper

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.580
a prescient work, so we kind of
went from bullshit to prescient

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:28.440
in that number of
years, and that's

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.920
kind of how the field changed.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:34.710
Of course, these papers--

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.400
I've talked about
just a few papers.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.170
There's many, many papers.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:41.190
They all build on each other.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:46.220
I don't like to
think one paper that

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:55.250
was the paper that did something
because they're all this work

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:58.410
builds on top of the
brilliant work before.

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:03.350
But so I just show a few
here of a timeline, though,

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:06.890
of how these things went.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:11.100
And yeah, the
interesting thing is

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:19.130
of how fast the use of neural
nets in NLP and machine learning

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:25.980
grew from this time,
say, 2008 to 2018.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.820
So there was four
papers in ICML 2008

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.500
with the words deep
or neural in them.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.870
And I'm coauthor on two of them.

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.870
And then there's
hundreds in ICML 2018.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:41.180
Obviously, this is a
neural network based

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:45.020
things are the majority today.

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:46.440
That's the paradigm of today.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.690
Of course, can't say what's
going to be in the future,

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:54.450
but it's looking like a
pretty powerful approach.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:54.950
Yeah.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:59.000
So, of course, we were
interested in pushing

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:02.250
this further and making
these models reason.

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:09.680
So in 2015 we devised this
set of tasks, very simple task

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:16.070
called baby tasks, which you
ask these simple questions given

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:18.230
little stories.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:21.890
But the neural nets
of the day LSTMs

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:25.070
actually couldn't solve them.

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:27.980
And so we were hoping
that they could spark

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:29.610
innovation of new methods.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:35.300
Around the same time the
LLM attention mechanism

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.500
was born in 2014.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.870
So [INAUDIBLE] Cho/Bengio paper.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:44.610
So that was built for
machine translation

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:50.720
where the you'd have a tension
between the target sentence

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.012
words and the source
sentence words

00:12:53.012 --> 00:12:54.470
so that you could
have an alignment

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:57.690
between the words
in the two languages

00:12:57.690 --> 00:12:59.040
so you could translate them.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:03.860
And you can see this
sort of attention map

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:05.870
picture in the middle
of the slide here.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:12.650
So yeah, what we showed was that
for these kind of baby reasoning

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:18.110
tasks, if we stack
these layers of tension,

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:22.110
then you could actually do
multiple reasoning steps.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:29.430
So if you've got a question
like where is John here?

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:31.200
So Daniel went to the bathroom.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:32.430
Mary traveled to the hallway.

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:34.170
John went to the bedroom.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.770
John traveled to the bathroom.

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:37.950
Mary went to the office.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:39.950
Where is john?

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:43.740
Then you could attend to
these correct sentences

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:44.490
to get that right.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:46.532
Actually, that one's pretty
easy because you only

00:13:46.532 --> 00:13:50.990
need to attend to John travel to
the bathroom, the last sentence.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:54.630
That's called one
supporting fact question.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.630
But then you have a two
supporting fact question

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:57.838
where it's a little trickier.

00:13:57.838 --> 00:14:00.570
So there, John dropped the milk.

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:01.770
John took the milk there.

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:03.720
Sandra went back
to the bathroom.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:04.980
John moved to the hallway.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:06.480
Mary went back to the bedroom.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.610
And the question is,
where is the milk?

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:13.370
So now you have to see
that the model will

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:16.640
attend on the first two layers.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:20.420
You can see the attention scores
here, and hop 1, hop 2, hop 3.

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:22.650
This is in the story to
two supporting facts.

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:24.360
You see, it attends
to the sentence.

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:25.980
John took the milk there.

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:28.610
And so now it knows
that John has the milk,

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:31.080
and then it can attend
to this next sentence,

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.500
John moved to the hallway,
in the further layers

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:36.550
of the neural network so that
it can answer that hallway

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:37.520
is the correct answer.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.940
So this was
basically showing how

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:44.920
in these very simple problems,
the tension mechanism when

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:48.010
stacked layer by
layer can give you

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:50.030
more sophisticated reasoning.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.880
And so this was built
into this neural network

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.380
at the time called
a memory network.

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:56.440
So it had these stacked
layers of attention query

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:58.360
and key embeddings,
position embeddings

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:02.920
that it knew which sentence
or token was being attended

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:05.650
to neural networks
between attention layers

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:07.810
and the final softmax layer.

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:10.180
And so in many
ways, it was a kind

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:12.880
of a predecessor to
the transformer, which

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.430
improved on this recipe
with some other things

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:17.890
like multi-head
attention, self-attention,

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:21.160
and normalization.

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:26.800
And yeah, that led us
to 2017 with the advent

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:27.950
of the transformer.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.070
And that's more or
less the architecture

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:31.940
we're using today
with some tweaks.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:35.230
And then Bert in 2018 showed
that a masked language

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:40.780
model with a transformer
could work very well.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:45.100
And yeah, I just wanted
to link that to a paper

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:49.660
from 2014 with
Sutskever, Vinyals,

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:57.880
and Quoc V. Le which was showing
that you could do sequence

00:15:57.880 --> 00:15:59.720
to sequence learning
with neural networks.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:06.710
And I think that wasn't
with attention maps yet.

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:09.340
But still the conclusions
from that paper

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.460
were essentially the
scaling hypothesis

00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:14.420
that we're using today.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:16.790
So you can see this
conclusion slide.

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:18.520
If you have a large
big data set and you

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:21.680
train a very big neural network,
then success is guaranteed.

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:22.850
It's a little bit glib.

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:26.450
But yeah, this scaling
hypothesis, essentially,

00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:29.290
when Ilya Sutskever
went to OpenAI,

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:36.240
they drove this hypothesis
making or GPT 1, 2,

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:38.390
3, 4 and so on.

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:41.870
These are bigger
models, more data,

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:44.340
and indeed this has worked.

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:47.780
So these are basically
the ingredients that

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:50.090
have taken us to some degree--

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:52.370
I saw LLMs everywhere,
so I asked actually

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:55.100
an LLM to give me
a list of LLMs that

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:56.885
have come out in year order.

00:16:56.885 --> 00:16:58.260
This doesn't
include all of them,

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:02.200
but you can see a bunch
that have come out here.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:11.329
But that's just
so far been mostly

00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:13.700
about language modeling and
the language model objective

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:14.369
function.

00:17:14.369 --> 00:17:16.950
And that isn't actually enough.

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:20.010
So we do need other ways
of training these models.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:22.790
And I would characterize
2020 onwards

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:26.490
as exploring that more deeply.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:30.220
Now we have these
better language models.

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:37.040
So 2019 actually, we did
a work on dialogue agents,

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:41.890
which we call the self-feeding
chatbot, where they could train

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:44.360
not only just with this
language model objective

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:49.160
but by using a reward model.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:52.030
So we train a reward
model, and then

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:55.180
during conversations
with humans,

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:59.620
if it thinks there's a large
reward for one of the things it

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:03.490
said, it can add that back
into the training set,

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:06.190
and then do
supervised fine tuning

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:08.690
on this ever-growing data set.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:11.200
So this was kind of a
pretty simple version

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:23.140
of what's being done
today with HF and RLAIF.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:28.470
Then in 2020 we released
this BlenderBot model

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:33.000
that was a pre-trained
language model up

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.080
to about 10 billion parameters.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:40.470
And then crucially, we don't
just use the language model

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.400
pretraining objective.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:45.450
We supervise, fine tune
that on dialogue data

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:48.090
which was human annotated.

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:56.620
And then when you
evaluated this with humans,

00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:58.410
they actually said
our best models

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:03.090
were about as engaging as the
average human and superior

00:19:03.090 --> 00:19:07.355
to another model from Google
called Meena at the time.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:14.760
But the actual recipes that
we use today, we do use

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:17.940
supervised fine tuning
today, but there's

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.050
some more sophisticated
stuff as well.

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:27.120
So in 2022, you had
the InstructGPT paper,

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:30.330
which advocated more
for reinforcement

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:31.720
learning from human feedback.

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:36.360
So what you do there is
you have this SFT stage

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:39.670
where you collect
demonstration data from humans.

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:40.780
That's step one.

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:44.620
But step two, you also
collect comparison data,

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:48.100
so which of these two
responses is better, A or B?

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:50.410
And then get humans
to rank those.

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:52.470
And then you train
a reward model.

00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:55.200
So similar to how we
had a reward model

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:57.010
in the self-feeding chatbot.

00:19:57.010 --> 00:20:02.100
But then you use reinforcement
learning with that reward model

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:08.040
to then generalize those
comparisons with the reward

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:13.390
model function to new
generations from your policy.

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:16.650
And try and make
your model better

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:21.420
by generating responses
and essentially judging

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.500
with this reward model
and fixing itself.

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:32.700
So a more of a step towards
this self-training goal

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:38.940
beyond the language
model training criterion.

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:42.100
And then there's an
alternative to RLHF.

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:46.500
Well, it's a little bit simpler
called direct preference

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:47.430
optimization.

00:20:47.430 --> 00:20:52.140
I mentioned because it's
also pretty popular, DPO.

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:56.440
So it says, OK, let's
not use a reward model.

00:20:56.440 --> 00:20:57.730
We've got preference data.

00:20:57.730 --> 00:21:00.930
We're just going
to directly push

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:05.980
the probability of the chosen
in a preference pair sort of up

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:10.030
and the probability of the
rejecting the preference pair.

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:12.280
So these would be two possible
outputs from the model,

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:14.700
push one up, push
one down so there's

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:17.010
a margin between the two.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:21.540
And in certain
experiments, the DPO

00:21:21.540 --> 00:21:27.030
works quite well
compared to PPO, RLHF.

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:30.850
And then SFT can also work
but generally a little worse,

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:34.680
but it depends on the setting.

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:37.500
And I think in some
cases, you really

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:43.530
do need the full reinforcement
learning because your policy is

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:45.002
going to change.

00:21:45.002 --> 00:21:50.640
So the DPO just with a fixed
preference data like that

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:53.190
can't take that into account.

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:56.580
But we'll see ways
of fixing that

00:21:56.580 --> 00:22:00.180
by making it iterative as well.

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:05.250
But overall, you choose one of
those methods SFT, RLHF or DPO

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:10.470
and fine tune your language
model with one of them,

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:13.270
and then you get pretty
good instruction following.

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:16.500
So there isn't necessarily
this explicit chain

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:19.080
of thought reasoning
yet, but compared

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:23.310
to the original language models,
you're doing really well.

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:26.820
So, for example, here this is
from the InstructGPT paper.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:27.990
You've got this prompt.

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:34.370
What's the purpose of this code
GPT 3 without the fine tuning

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:35.730
doesn't do a very good job.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:38.300
It instructs-- GPT does a much
better job on the right hand

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:38.990
side.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:43.490
So, obviously, yeah, today
we all know quite a lot

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:48.650
about using these ChatGPT
type models as we're mostly--

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:53.720
yeah, a lot of the population
is using them at this stage.

00:22:53.720 --> 00:23:01.700
So OK, that got me up
to basically having

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:04.380
a really good system 1 model.

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:07.070
It takes in this input,
so this instruction.

00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:11.300
It directly spits out an output,
but it isn't doing the system 2

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:12.210
reasoning.

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:16.670
So that's the next step.

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:23.640
And yeah, between, say, 2022 and
2023, the approach to doing that

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:26.940
was what I would call
a prompting approach.

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:29.380
And I'm going to talk
about those first,

00:23:29.380 --> 00:23:32.670
and then we're going to
look at more modern things

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:36.240
from the last year
or so where we're

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:40.050
going to more train
directly to do this system 2

00:23:40.050 --> 00:23:42.480
reasoning instead.

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:49.020
So chain of thought prompting
the initial papers they

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:50.920
did that in a couple of ways.

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:55.720
One was by what's called
few-shot prompting.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:58.240
You would have say
for some maths task,

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:01.260
you would have
examples of the kind

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:04.170
of chain-of-though reasoning
you would like to see.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:09.370
Say if you had a no
eight shot examples,

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:14.460
you'd have eight examples
of doing reasoning.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:18.210
That's shown here with
the-- instead of just saying

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:22.090
the answer is 11 as
in standard prompting,

00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:24.250
you would have rather
started with 5 balls.

00:24:24.250 --> 00:24:26.910
2 cans of 3 tennis balls
each is 6 tennis balls.

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:28.180
5 plus 6 equals 11.

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:29.260
The answer is 11.

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:33.610
So that's the chain of thought
there that's highlighted.

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:36.040
So you'd have examples
of that in the prompt,

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:39.460
and now you would generate
from the language model.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:43.230
And it's going to mimic
that few-shot prompting

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.460
and use a chain of thought.

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:49.290
And so that was kind of
one of the first approaches

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:49.990
of prompting.

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:53.850
And then in the other paper
here large language models

00:24:53.850 --> 00:24:55.420
are zero-shot reasoners.

00:24:55.420 --> 00:24:59.440
They showed actually
it can be even simpler.

00:24:59.440 --> 00:25:02.650
You could just say, let's think
step by step in the prompt.

00:25:02.650 --> 00:25:06.600
And that could still
bring about these steps

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:08.580
or these chains of
thought could happen

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:12.480
that it could generate before
it generates the final response.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:17.760
And you get really big
improvements on math tasks.

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:26.360
So you could go from say 10%
on GSM 8K up to 40% or 50%.

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:31.580
So yeah, that's
really spurred people

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:36.420
to push the system to
approaches further.

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:41.440
And it's not just math
tasks that that works for.

00:25:43.970 --> 00:25:48.290
So you can also look at the
other failures of system

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:50.720
1 of the base language model.

00:25:50.720 --> 00:25:54.930
It also has a factuality
and hallucination issues.

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:58.130
So here's an example query,
name some politicians

00:25:58.130 --> 00:25:59.790
who were born in New York.

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:02.090
And the third one
it lists here--

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:03.650
this is from ChatGPT--

00:26:03.650 --> 00:26:08.060
is Michael Bloomberg
which is incorrect.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:13.760
So you can try and set up a
system 2 reasoning type chain

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:15.480
of thought to fix this problem.

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:16.800
So it's not a math problem.

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:22.580
So that's a paper we did called
chain of verification where

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:26.700
the idea is you get this
first baseline response.

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:28.830
You can think of that
now as your draft.

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:30.540
So it's not actually
your final response.

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.100
It's part of your
chain of thought.

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:37.190
And then given that
draft, the language model

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:42.000
asks itself more questions
to check its own draft.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:45.390
So it could ask, where
was Hillary Clinton born?

00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:46.590
Where was Donald Trump born?

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:48.270
Where is Michael Bloomberg born?

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:51.780
And then answer those
again as single questions.

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:56.990
Now, the answer might be
different than writing out

00:26:56.990 --> 00:27:01.730
this whole list from the name,
some politicians question.

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:03.822
Because it's
phrased differently,

00:27:03.822 --> 00:27:06.030
you actually get a different
response from the model.

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:09.440
And models tend to
be better actually

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:14.650
at these single question
answer pairs that

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:18.550
are short compared to these
long responses they write.

00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:21.760
They're more likely to get
something wrong somewhere

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:23.030
in the long response.

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:25.990
So when you plan
those verifications

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:28.610
of those verification
questions and execute them,

00:27:28.610 --> 00:27:32.090
you can actually get the model
to see its own discrepancy.

00:27:32.090 --> 00:27:34.240
So now it actually knows
that Michael Bloomberg

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:37.540
was born in Boston,
even though it's

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:40.150
included him in the
list of politicians

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:41.930
who are born in New York.

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:46.900
And now you can make it
cross-check itself and see

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:52.870
that it was wrong, and write
a final verified response

00:27:52.870 --> 00:27:54.800
and treat the old
one as a draft.

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:56.930
And now it gets that correct.

00:27:56.930 --> 00:28:04.630
So that's a somewhat
different chain

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:07.610
of thought system 2 reasoning
compared to the math problems.

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:16.010
And yeah, in that work we showed
that on various knowledge tasks,

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:18.520
you can get large
performance in precision

00:28:18.520 --> 00:28:24.670
like three times as
good with that approach.

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:27.760
So yeah.

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:32.560
And you could think,
oh, that's sort of--

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:39.280
maybe because that paper was
from a little while ago that it

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:44.465
might be fixed today, but
actually, I tried on ChatGPT 4o,

00:28:44.465 --> 00:28:46.340
and it's still making
these kind of mistakes.

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:51.260
Like here I did a query list,
30 most famous women in AI,

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:53.390
and it included Yoshua
Bengio in the list

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:55.880
or what prominent
women work at OpenAI

00:28:55.880 --> 00:29:00.080
and it said Ilya Sutskever
as a first in the list.

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:05.380
So still something that's
definitely needs to be fixed.

00:29:05.380 --> 00:29:09.080
And you can fix it with this
sort of system 2 reasoning.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:12.610
I think actually
[? O1 ?] does better.

00:29:12.610 --> 00:29:14.325
But yeah.

00:29:18.310 --> 00:29:22.030
And there's other things you can
do with this system 2 reasoning

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:22.960
as well.

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:26.680
So here's another problem
with language models

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:33.500
that they have what's called
semantic leakage and sycophancy.

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:38.560
So when you do attention
because it's a soft attention,

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:40.910
it's not sharp.

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:46.100
You're assigning probabilities
across the context.

00:29:46.100 --> 00:29:50.930
So there'll be all
these non-zero values.

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:54.590
And it means the whole context
can affect the LLM output.

00:29:54.590 --> 00:30:00.070
And this can be bad
because, even basically

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:02.890
we see irrelevant
parts which look

00:30:02.890 --> 00:30:06.590
sort of superficially correlated
tend to have an effect.

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:10.460
So, for example, a prompt
like, he likes ants.

00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:14.200
His favorite food is-- can get
completed by the language model

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:18.500
as ant-covered chocolate, which
isn't particularly desirable.

00:30:18.500 --> 00:30:19.220
He likes yellow.

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:22.330
He works as a school bus driver.

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:25.913
That's from Genen, et al in 24.

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:31.970
I see this as a similar
problem, the sycophancy problem.

00:30:31.970 --> 00:30:34.790
If you say something
like as an instruction,

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:37.550
I think that the sun is
yellow when viewed from space,

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:38.390
what do you think?

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:42.340
So you prime with
the wrong answer

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:46.810
because actually, that's an
illusion caused by atmosphere.

00:30:46.810 --> 00:30:49.130
Then it will actually agree.

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:53.290
The LLM tends to agree with you
and say yes, the sun is actually

00:30:53.290 --> 00:30:55.760
yellow when viewed from space.

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:58.480
So I see that as
a similar problem

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:01.180
that basically you've got
that text in the context,

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:05.450
and it tends to attend to
it and get things wrong.

00:31:05.450 --> 00:31:07.590
So another example,
if you add some facts

00:31:07.590 --> 00:31:10.330
about some city in the
context, like Sunnyvale,

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:12.550
and then you ask for
someone's birthplace,

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:17.460
it's likely to say
Sunnyvale as the answer.

00:31:17.460 --> 00:31:21.970
Yeah, I just typed
in this one today.

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:26.230
Did OpenAI have a model called
self-feeding at one time?

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:30.600
And ChatGPT 4o replied, yes,
OpenAI introduced the concept

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:33.730
of self-feeding in the
Self-feeding Chatbot paper,

00:31:33.730 --> 00:31:36.160
which that wasn't
actually from OpenAI.

00:31:36.160 --> 00:31:38.740
That was from
Facebook AI Research.

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:40.990
So again, you can
still prime these.

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:45.030
This is a current day problem.

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:46.320
So how can you fix that?

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:47.650
With system 2 reasoning.

00:31:47.650 --> 00:31:50.843
So there was a paper called
System 2 Attention we did last--

00:31:53.490 --> 00:31:59.670
that was end of
2023, I think, where

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:03.100
you do this chain
of thought reasoning

00:32:03.100 --> 00:32:07.560
which attempts to rewrite
the original instruction

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:08.890
and remove the bias.

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:10.750
So again, this is
done by prompting.

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:13.980
So essentially, the prompt is to
give the original instruction,

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:17.520
and then ask the language
model, rewrite this instruction.

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:21.190
Remove the irrelevant
parts and the bias.

00:32:21.190 --> 00:32:23.140
And then you'll get
a new instruction.

00:32:23.140 --> 00:32:24.990
And then you get
the language model

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:27.250
to answer the
rewritten instruction.

00:32:27.250 --> 00:32:29.410
So you want to get
rid of this bias.

00:32:29.410 --> 00:32:32.680
And then you can
get better results.

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:35.260
You're not biased as
much by this issue.

00:32:35.260 --> 00:32:38.040
So, for example, I
think the sun is yellow

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:40.030
when viewed from space.

00:32:40.030 --> 00:32:40.920
What do you think?

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:44.670
When you use this
chain-of-thought system 2

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:48.210
attention, to rewrite that,
it would rewrite it as,

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:50.980
I'm curious about the color of
the sun when viewed from space.

00:32:50.980 --> 00:32:53.320
Can you provide
information on this topic?

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:59.340
So to remove the yellow which
was biasing the question

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:01.290
to a certain answer.

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:10.810
And yeah, this works also on
math problems if there's say,

00:33:10.810 --> 00:33:12.810
there's parts of
the math problem

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:17.850
which are completely irrelevant
they can actually fool a model,

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:20.970
and it will try
and remove those.

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:25.680
So yeah, this is yeah,
just to show you really

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:29.050
that chain of thought
and system 2 reasoning

00:33:29.050 --> 00:33:30.400
is not just for math problems.

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:33.300
There's a lot of
things you a model

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:36.900
can think about with
these intermediate tokens

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:41.460
and fix a lot of problems
in all kinds of tasks

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:43.410
that it's trying to solve.

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:47.020
This last one I show it's
the task of evaluation.

00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:51.120
So you're given a question
like an instruction

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:52.780
and two possible responses.

00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:55.620
You want the model to say
which of the two responses

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:59.970
is better, which is a really
important thing for training

00:33:59.970 --> 00:34:02.910
as we show, because
you could basically

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:04.680
use that as a reward model.

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:07.570
And it's also important
for evaluation as well.

00:34:07.570 --> 00:34:11.320
But yeah, you can also do
system 2 reasoning there.

00:34:11.320 --> 00:34:15.330
So in this paper,
Branch-Solve-Merge,

00:34:15.330 --> 00:34:20.170
the way that was done was again
through prompting by asking,

00:34:20.170 --> 00:34:25.530
given that instruction to
first ask the language model

00:34:25.530 --> 00:34:30.420
to break down its evaluation
into different criteria

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:33.340
such as relevance, clarity,
accuracy, originality.

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:38.790
And it would come up with those
criteria specific to the task

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:39.969
that it's trying to solve.

00:34:39.969 --> 00:34:45.570
And then to generate an
evaluation independently

00:34:45.570 --> 00:34:49.600
for each of those
different branches.

00:34:49.600 --> 00:34:51.790
And then merge them
back together again.

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:55.469
And it was shown that could
give better evaluation results

00:34:55.469 --> 00:34:59.480
through the extra thinking time
basically that the language

00:34:59.480 --> 00:35:01.580
model has.

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:07.010
So yeah, that was-- there's
many other works as well during

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:07.710
that period.

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:12.260
But I just wanted to give
an overview of a few of them

00:35:12.260 --> 00:35:15.350
to show there was a lot of
prompting approaches that

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:18.290
showed that spending
time thinking could

00:35:18.290 --> 00:35:20.360
get you better results.

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:27.380
But what we really want to do is
try and train these models end

00:35:27.380 --> 00:35:30.030
to end to be able to
be good at reasoning,

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:31.580
not like just prompting.

00:35:31.580 --> 00:35:36.090
So that I'd say the next
wave after that is--

00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:37.910
and we're in it right now--

00:35:37.910 --> 00:35:41.960
is improving this reasoning
through optimization

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:44.990
and so better reasoning
via self-improvement.

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:50.870
And to get there,
I'm going to start

00:35:50.870 --> 00:35:54.380
with the self-rewarding
language models

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:59.450
that I was talking about right
at the beginning of the talk.

00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:03.260
And so this is a method
where the LLM proves itself

00:36:03.260 --> 00:36:11.580
by assigning rewards to its own
outputs and optimizing itself.

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:18.800
So yeah, where the hope is that
this could lead to it becoming

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:21.510
smarter at each iteration.

00:36:21.510 --> 00:36:27.680
And yeah, so the
research question

00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:31.970
is, can that make it superhuman?

00:36:31.970 --> 00:36:36.780
So the standard reinforcement
learning from human feedback,

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:40.100
which I mentioned earlier in the
talk, uses humans in the loop,

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:43.730
first to create the
supervised fine tuning

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:46.260
data, so the instructions
and their responses,

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:48.210
and then to collect
these judgments.

00:36:48.210 --> 00:36:52.580
So which of these two
responses is better, A or B?

00:36:52.580 --> 00:36:58.680
Now, yeah, that's shown in this
picture that I showed before.

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:00.960
So there's two spots
where there's a human.

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:04.960
And the problem
is language models

00:37:04.960 --> 00:37:06.210
are getting better and better.

00:37:06.210 --> 00:37:11.810
So it's just getting a
really tricky job for humans

00:37:11.810 --> 00:37:18.060
to be as good as we
need for these tasks.

00:37:18.060 --> 00:37:20.720
Humans need to read these
responses really carefully

00:37:20.720 --> 00:37:23.810
in order to make decisions.

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:27.350
So, for example,
now that our LLMs

00:37:27.350 --> 00:37:36.800
are really good at writing
code or no sophisticated math

00:37:36.800 --> 00:37:40.080
outputs, even getting
good at like--

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:42.140
I mean, maybe not perfect
yet, but getting good

00:37:42.140 --> 00:37:45.270
at legal or medical
outputs, things like this.

00:37:45.270 --> 00:37:47.960
The problem is, it's harder
and harder for humans

00:37:47.960 --> 00:37:50.790
to be good labelers
for these tasks.

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:54.540
You might need some of
the best mathematicians

00:37:54.540 --> 00:37:57.390
and human mathematicians
in the world

00:37:57.390 --> 00:38:01.110
to label whether these
responses are correct or not.

00:38:01.110 --> 00:38:04.020
And similarly, you need
really good coders.

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:06.130
Or even if you don't
need them today,

00:38:06.130 --> 00:38:08.070
you'll certainly
need them tomorrow.

00:38:08.070 --> 00:38:12.580
And so the average human that
you pick from the population,

00:38:12.580 --> 00:38:14.700
then they're just not
going to be good enough

00:38:14.700 --> 00:38:18.930
at these specialized tasks and
be able to annotate this data.

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:25.080
So this is what motivates
trying to get the language

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:27.360
model to try and help itself.

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:30.390
And so that's
basically otherwise

00:38:30.390 --> 00:38:34.410
how can we continue improving
a model if it's already

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:38.190
fairly superhuman compared
to the average human

00:38:38.190 --> 00:38:39.960
that you could pick?

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:44.340
So there's two observations
that can help us solve this.

00:38:44.340 --> 00:38:48.630
Observation 1 is that we know
language models can continue

00:38:48.630 --> 00:38:53.000
improving if they're given good
judgments on response quality,

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:55.570
because that's what we're
shown in reinforcement learning

00:38:55.570 --> 00:38:59.270
from human feedback or in
something like DPO as well.

00:38:59.270 --> 00:39:03.410
So if you have good preferences
of chosen and rejected pairs,

00:39:03.410 --> 00:39:06.250
then you can improve the model
by training and pushing up

00:39:06.250 --> 00:39:09.760
the probability of the chosens
and down the probability

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:10.520
to reject it.

00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:14.050
And the same thing
for a reward model

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:17.080
with reinforcement learning.

00:39:17.080 --> 00:39:20.590
And observation 2
is that we actually

00:39:20.590 --> 00:39:24.430
have now language models that
can provide good judgments

00:39:24.430 --> 00:39:25.520
on model generation.

00:39:25.520 --> 00:39:28.810
So I was showing you the
Branch-Solve-Merge approach

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:29.810
just earlier.

00:39:29.810 --> 00:39:32.470
So that's an approach
for a language model

00:39:32.470 --> 00:39:40.180
basically to give a judgment on
two responses as an instruction

00:39:40.180 --> 00:39:41.300
following task.

00:39:41.300 --> 00:39:48.160
So this is called LLM-as-a-Judge
typically where for the language

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:53.140
model it's not any different
a task than all the other

00:39:53.140 --> 00:39:57.370
instruction following tasks it
has to do like write some code,

00:39:57.370 --> 00:40:00.140
or respond to this
particular question.

00:40:00.140 --> 00:40:02.710
And in particular
this question we're

00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:06.341
asking is, which of these two
responses is better, A or B?

00:40:06.341 --> 00:40:10.060
And there's a bunch of work
showing LLM-as-a-Judge is

00:40:10.060 --> 00:40:12.560
actually a pretty good method.

00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:15.200
So how about combining
these two things together.

00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:21.730
So we replace the human
feedback with the LLM feedback.

00:40:21.730 --> 00:40:25.990
So the key idea then is to train
this so-called self-rewarding

00:40:25.990 --> 00:40:26.780
language model.

00:40:26.780 --> 00:40:30.080
It's going to be its own
judge as its training.

00:40:30.080 --> 00:40:33.880
So it needs to have this
instruction following capability

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:36.680
for all the kinds of user
instructions we want,

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:39.430
but one of those
user instructions

00:40:39.430 --> 00:40:43.970
is actually the evaluation
task that we're interested in.

00:40:43.970 --> 00:40:45.290
So it looks like this.

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:47.080
Here is an instruction.

00:40:47.080 --> 00:40:49.040
Here is a model of response.

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:53.000
And then the task is
can you assign a score,

00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:54.082
say between 0 and 5?

00:40:54.082 --> 00:40:55.540
There'd be other
ways of doing that

00:40:55.540 --> 00:40:59.530
as well but to this response
and then based on some rubric.

00:40:59.530 --> 00:41:01.990
And then you would expect,
say, chain-of-thought reasoning

00:41:01.990 --> 00:41:04.480
process and then
a final judgment

00:41:04.480 --> 00:41:06.380
of the score of the response.

00:41:06.380 --> 00:41:10.000
So if we have these
two things, then we

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:13.760
can make this model train
itself and be self-rewarding.

00:41:13.760 --> 00:41:17.200
So we also go through
an iterative process

00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:20.800
of data creation and
curation so that we

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:25.390
close this whole training loop
where we train on this new data.

00:41:25.390 --> 00:41:27.940
And then the goal
is the model is

00:41:27.940 --> 00:41:30.430
going to get better in terms
of both instruction following

00:41:30.430 --> 00:41:35.210
and evaluation
ability in each cycle.

00:41:35.210 --> 00:41:38.240
And we empirically
showed that was possible.

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:43.060
So this was right at the
beginning of last year.

00:41:43.060 --> 00:41:45.850
So that was the picture
that I was showing right

00:41:45.850 --> 00:41:50.110
at the beginning of the talk.

00:41:50.110 --> 00:41:54.230
So yeah, we did these
experiments on LLAMA-2-70B.

00:41:57.160 --> 00:42:01.300
And we used Open Assistant
as a sort of seed,

00:42:01.300 --> 00:42:04.270
so that seed instruction data.

00:42:04.270 --> 00:42:09.160
And it also has some
seed evaluation data.

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:14.540
So the LLM-as-a-Judge prompts
that we use looks like this.

00:42:14.540 --> 00:42:19.450
So that's asking it
to do chain of thought

00:42:19.450 --> 00:42:25.090
and then score out of 5
a particular instruction.

00:42:25.090 --> 00:42:28.640
And then we're going to go
through this training loop.

00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:34.640
So the first part is it's
going to generate new tasks.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:38.630
So we use something called
self-instruct to do that.

00:42:38.630 --> 00:42:42.800
So basically, you start
with your seed instructions,

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:44.930
which are from humans.

00:42:44.930 --> 00:42:49.230
And you use few-shot prompting
with a few of those instructions

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:53.370
to ask the language model to
generate a new instruction.

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:56.730
And then you can do this
hundreds or thousands of times

00:42:56.730 --> 00:42:59.050
to get a whole new set of tasks.

00:42:59.050 --> 00:43:02.920
Then for those tasks you
can generate responses.

00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:05.610
So you'll generate multiple
candidate responses

00:43:05.610 --> 00:43:11.740
for each prompt or task, so
let's say four in our case.

00:43:11.740 --> 00:43:15.780
And then you generate rewards
with the LLM-as-a-Judge prompt

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:18.750
that I just showed to give
you those scores for each

00:43:18.750 --> 00:43:20.320
of the four responses.

00:43:20.320 --> 00:43:23.230
And from that you can
generate preference pairs.

00:43:23.230 --> 00:43:26.950
So you take the highest
scoring of the four responses,

00:43:26.950 --> 00:43:29.590
and that would be
the chosen for DPO,

00:43:29.590 --> 00:43:31.570
and the lowest scoring
would be the rejected.

00:43:31.570 --> 00:43:34.410
You can carry on
training your model now

00:43:34.410 --> 00:43:38.800
with this DPO training, and
then repeat the whole cycle.

00:43:38.800 --> 00:43:41.320
Now you have a better
model M t plus 1.

00:43:41.320 --> 00:43:44.940
You can go back,
generate new prompts

00:43:44.940 --> 00:43:48.310
to generate new responses,
generate new rewards, and so on.

00:43:48.310 --> 00:43:52.140
And we do this loop three
times in the experiments

00:43:52.140 --> 00:43:53.110
in that paper.

00:43:53.110 --> 00:43:55.170
And we're going to
evaluate the performance

00:43:55.170 --> 00:43:59.010
on two axes, the ability
to follow instructions

00:43:59.010 --> 00:44:02.130
and its ability
as a reward model.

00:44:02.130 --> 00:44:07.770
There's some benchmarks called
AlpacaEval 2.0 and MT-Bench

00:44:07.770 --> 00:44:12.960
at the time that we used and
another test set of prompts.

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:17.190
And we use GPT 4 evaluation
and human evaluation

00:44:17.190 --> 00:44:20.700
to check how this model
performed through the three

00:44:20.700 --> 00:44:22.870
iterations of self-rewarding.

00:44:22.870 --> 00:44:28.290
And you can see that they agree
with each other humans and GPT 4

00:44:28.290 --> 00:44:32.380
that we compare it to doing
supervised fine tuning.

00:44:32.380 --> 00:44:35.290
On each iteration, the
model is getting better.

00:44:35.290 --> 00:44:40.860
So it's getting better
than the SFT baseline.

00:44:40.860 --> 00:44:46.380
And yeah, so that's the
power of this self-training.

00:44:46.380 --> 00:44:50.880
And we can look at the
win rate on AlpacaEval 2.0

00:44:50.880 --> 00:44:53.280
And this paper got quite a
bit of attention at the time

00:44:53.280 --> 00:44:59.400
because this win rate for
such a sort of simple model

00:44:59.400 --> 00:45:00.430
was quite good.

00:45:00.430 --> 00:45:03.030
It started from
this LLAMA-2-70B.

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:07.050
And the win rate went up
from 10% to 15% to 20%

00:45:07.050 --> 00:45:10.440
in the three iterations,
which put it almost on par

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:14.100
with a version of GPT 4.

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:19.410
And this was just done
by the first author

00:45:19.410 --> 00:45:22.810
is Weijia Yuan, who's
a PhD student at NYU.

00:45:22.810 --> 00:45:26.110
So there wasn't like a big
team building this project

00:45:26.110 --> 00:45:30.270
but the algorithm
works quite well.

00:45:30.270 --> 00:45:35.190
And yeah, so you could look at
what is actually improving at.

00:45:35.190 --> 00:45:40.960
And it does improve
on all kinds of skills

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:44.590
but particularly more
things like humanities,

00:45:44.590 --> 00:45:46.800
extraction, STEM role
play and writing more

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:48.330
than it improves
at, say, something

00:45:48.330 --> 00:45:50.140
like math, code, and reasoning.

00:45:50.140 --> 00:45:54.570
And the reason why that would
be is because the LLM-as-a-Judge

00:45:54.570 --> 00:45:57.120
might not be as good at
evaluating math, code,

00:45:57.120 --> 00:45:59.340
and reasoning, and so it's
harder for it to improve

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:02.310
on those.

00:46:02.310 --> 00:46:05.010
This is a breakdown
of these topics.

00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:08.760
You can see that the gap
between the iterations

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:13.440
is smaller for things like
math, which is on the far right.

00:46:13.440 --> 00:46:16.110
And then you can measure
the ability of this model

00:46:16.110 --> 00:46:19.240
as an evaluator.

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:25.860
And one way of measuring that
is by looking at its correlation

00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:27.160
with human judgments.

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:30.580
So the pairwise accuracy-- we
had a whole bunch of metrics

00:46:30.580 --> 00:46:35.280
for the pairwise accuracy
metric would show it's agreement

00:46:35.280 --> 00:46:39.350
with humans on whether
A or B the right way

00:46:39.350 --> 00:46:40.740
around or the wrong way around.

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:43.380
And you can see that
it does increase,

00:46:43.380 --> 00:46:45.680
not by a massive amount
but it does increase

00:46:45.680 --> 00:46:47.610
over the three iterations.

00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:52.550
So it is actually getting better
at being a reward model, which

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:56.780
I think is exciting, because
if the model is getting better

00:46:56.780 --> 00:47:00.450
at making judgments of
what's right or wrong,

00:47:00.450 --> 00:47:03.770
then it's getting better
at rewarding itself

00:47:03.770 --> 00:47:05.760
at following instructions.

00:47:05.760 --> 00:47:09.460
So there's a virtuous circle
going on there to some extent.

00:47:09.460 --> 00:47:13.020
Of course it plateaus
in experiments,

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:17.120
but it still gives it the
capacity to self-train

00:47:17.120 --> 00:47:22.070
to get better than the
initial human annotations

00:47:22.070 --> 00:47:24.440
that it had at the beginning.

00:47:24.440 --> 00:47:28.370
Now, as I said, it
didn't improve as much

00:47:28.370 --> 00:47:29.760
on reasoning tasks.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:33.500
So the next thing we wanted
to ask was how can we

00:47:33.500 --> 00:47:35.160
make it improve on those?

00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:40.020
And yeah, so in
April of last year,

00:47:40.020 --> 00:47:43.730
we did this work called
Iterative Reasoning Preference

00:47:43.730 --> 00:47:48.980
Optimization, which you can
see from the picture it's

00:47:48.980 --> 00:47:52.490
a similar pipeline,
but now we're

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:54.060
going to generate
chain of thought.

00:47:54.060 --> 00:47:56.847
So in the experiment I
just showed you there

00:47:56.847 --> 00:47:57.930
were no chain of thoughts.

00:47:57.930 --> 00:48:01.340
It was just a system 1 language
model output the answers

00:48:01.340 --> 00:48:03.530
for instruction following.

00:48:03.530 --> 00:48:07.760
But in this version, we're going
to generate train of thoughts

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:09.120
and then answers.

00:48:09.120 --> 00:48:10.830
And we're going to
compute rewards.

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:15.270
And otherwise the
pipeline is the same.

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:19.850
Now the rewards aren't going
to be done by LLM-as-a-Judge

00:48:19.850 --> 00:48:27.600
because we don't trust the LLM
on say these math tasks as much.

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:30.330
I mean, I think that's
a subject of research.

00:48:30.330 --> 00:48:35.310
Can we get them to improve by
trusting their own judgments.

00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:37.490
But in this work we
just said, OK, we've

00:48:37.490 --> 00:48:40.820
got a bunch of
math training data

00:48:40.820 --> 00:48:42.950
where we know the
right answer so let's

00:48:42.950 --> 00:48:44.700
just use that as a reward.

00:48:44.700 --> 00:48:48.620
And this is being
commonly called

00:48:48.620 --> 00:48:53.900
in the last weeks is like a
verifiable reward for a math

00:48:53.900 --> 00:48:55.100
problem.

00:48:55.100 --> 00:48:58.010
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:48:58.010 --> 00:49:01.220
going to generate
the chain of thought

00:49:01.220 --> 00:49:03.380
and then the final answer.

00:49:03.380 --> 00:49:07.790
And then we're going to extract
the final answer from the LLM

00:49:07.790 --> 00:49:10.710
response, so remove the
chain of thought part.

00:49:10.710 --> 00:49:12.770
Just get the final
answer, and then just

00:49:12.770 --> 00:49:17.370
do a match with the answer that
we know for the math problem.

00:49:17.370 --> 00:49:19.560
So you can do that with
this kind of prompt.

00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:22.200
So where you give
the LLM the prompt.

00:49:22.200 --> 00:49:24.240
Your task is to answer
the question below.

00:49:24.240 --> 00:49:25.830
Give step-by-step reasoning.

00:49:25.830 --> 00:49:28.430
And when you're ready, please
use the format, final answer,

00:49:28.430 --> 00:49:29.780
colon.

00:49:29.780 --> 00:49:34.250
And then you can just simply
do this heuristic extraction

00:49:34.250 --> 00:49:37.010
where you search for
final answer, colon.

00:49:37.010 --> 00:49:38.475
Grab the text afterwards.

00:49:38.475 --> 00:49:39.600
That should be your answer.

00:49:39.600 --> 00:49:43.370
And now just do a string match
or some other fairly simple

00:49:43.370 --> 00:49:47.840
match with the answer,
the known gold answer.

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:54.080
And that's how you can
build your DPO pairs

00:49:54.080 --> 00:49:58.680
and improve your model and
then again iterate round.

00:49:58.680 --> 00:50:00.740
And that's the iteration,
again, is important

00:50:00.740 --> 00:50:02.450
because now we've
got a better model

00:50:02.450 --> 00:50:03.870
answering these math problems.

00:50:03.870 --> 00:50:05.990
We're going to generate
new chain of thoughts

00:50:05.990 --> 00:50:07.230
with the better model.

00:50:07.230 --> 00:50:11.360
Compute the rewards and
go around in a loop.

00:50:11.360 --> 00:50:17.600
And we showed on math
problems like GSM8K

00:50:17.600 --> 00:50:20.990
that that's working really well.

00:50:20.990 --> 00:50:25.060
So yeah you can see these
improvements across iteration 1

00:50:25.060 --> 00:50:31.710
to 4 is gaining a little
bit less than 10% there

00:50:31.710 --> 00:50:36.670
and also improves on some
multi-choice problems

00:50:36.670 --> 00:50:40.110
on ARC challenge and on these
harder math problems as well.

00:50:42.880 --> 00:50:48.520
And yeah, we just showed
that the DPO training

00:50:48.520 --> 00:50:49.760
is really important.

00:50:49.760 --> 00:50:53.720
You can't just use supervised
fine tuning with this training.

00:50:53.720 --> 00:50:57.460
You really need the negative
examples to be pushed down.

00:50:57.460 --> 00:50:58.845
Otherwise, it doesn't work.

00:51:01.450 --> 00:51:08.630
And yeah, so in 2024, September
or so, OpenAI's O1 came out.

00:51:08.630 --> 00:51:12.590
Of course, the exact
method is unknown.

00:51:12.590 --> 00:51:16.635
They didn't publish a paper
about it, but they did have--

00:51:16.635 --> 00:51:19.010
well, they didn't even show
the chain of thoughts either.

00:51:19.010 --> 00:51:20.440
From the model
there was a summary

00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:21.530
of the chain of thoughts.

00:51:21.530 --> 00:51:23.050
I think they still do that.

00:51:23.050 --> 00:51:27.940
But it was capable of answering
some quite sophisticated

00:51:27.940 --> 00:51:30.340
reasoning and math problems.

00:51:30.340 --> 00:51:34.330
And everyone was wondering,
how did they do it?

00:51:34.330 --> 00:51:36.490
Did they use some
kind of process reward

00:51:36.490 --> 00:51:39.800
model that was evaluating
the in-between steps?

00:51:39.800 --> 00:51:44.830
Did they use MCTS or some really
sophisticated search mechanisms

00:51:44.830 --> 00:51:48.850
to train with
reinforcement learning?

00:51:48.850 --> 00:51:52.840
A lot of ideas were posted on
Twitter of how they do this.

00:51:52.840 --> 00:51:59.630
And then DeepSeek R1 came
out just this January.

00:51:59.630 --> 00:52:03.290
And Marc Chen from
OpenAI tweeted,

00:52:03.290 --> 00:52:06.210
congrats to DeepSeek on
producing O1 level reasoning

00:52:06.210 --> 00:52:06.710
model.

00:52:06.710 --> 00:52:08.110
Their research
paper demonstrates

00:52:08.110 --> 00:52:10.750
they've independently found
some of the core ideas

00:52:10.750 --> 00:52:12.740
that we did on our way to O1.

00:52:12.740 --> 00:52:18.460
OK, so then that means assuming
that OpenAI used the same stuff

00:52:18.460 --> 00:52:20.710
as DeepSeek, we have
a deep seat paper,

00:52:20.710 --> 00:52:23.680
so we can look at what they did.

00:52:23.680 --> 00:52:29.170
And in some respects
it's quite similar

00:52:29.170 --> 00:52:32.380
to the iterative reasoning
preference optimization

00:52:32.380 --> 00:52:36.880
that we did earlier in 2024
that I just showed you.

00:52:36.880 --> 00:52:41.500
So they have a prompt,
which asks it to think

00:52:41.500 --> 00:52:44.540
and then to put the
answer in a box.

00:52:44.540 --> 00:52:48.200
You see this answer
here in the prompt.

00:52:48.200 --> 00:52:50.780
And then they extract that.

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:56.500
And then they use a rule-based
verification of correctness

00:52:56.500 --> 00:52:59.330
with verifiable rewards.

00:52:59.330 --> 00:53:03.110
And they apply that in a loop
generating chain of thoughts,

00:53:03.110 --> 00:53:05.470
checking just the
correctness of the answer,

00:53:05.470 --> 00:53:07.490
and then rewarding
this correctness.

00:53:07.490 --> 00:53:11.900
They use a different
optimization technique.

00:53:11.900 --> 00:53:13.700
They don't use iterative PPO.

00:53:13.700 --> 00:53:16.150
They use GRPO.

00:53:16.150 --> 00:53:21.020
But otherwise, this recipe
looks kind of like that.

00:53:21.020 --> 00:53:25.010
It's just done
much bigger scale.

00:53:25.010 --> 00:53:27.020
So in the experiments
I showed you

00:53:27.020 --> 00:53:30.190
we just had one data set GSM8K.

00:53:30.190 --> 00:53:33.680
So key here is start from
a really strong big model.

00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:38.410
I think it's a 600 and
something parameter model.

00:53:38.410 --> 00:53:43.880
671 billion parameter
model DeepSeek 3.

00:53:43.880 --> 00:53:47.620
And then you have a large
amount of this reasoning

00:53:47.620 --> 00:53:49.700
data with verifiable rewards.

00:53:49.700 --> 00:53:56.050
And you train with this RL
generating chain of thoughts

00:53:56.050 --> 00:53:58.000
and rewarding the
ones that it gets

00:53:58.000 --> 00:54:01.490
right, and basically, exploring
the space of chain of thoughts.

00:54:01.490 --> 00:54:04.540
And they observe that
the chain of thoughts

00:54:04.540 --> 00:54:09.100
get longer over training as
it finds more sophisticated

00:54:09.100 --> 00:54:10.700
reasoning techniques.

00:54:10.700 --> 00:54:13.810
And then the final
model does very well

00:54:13.810 --> 00:54:19.060
on these code and math problems,
similar to OpenAI's O1 model.

00:54:19.060 --> 00:54:24.230
And this actually was a
big, big news outside of AI.

00:54:24.230 --> 00:54:29.450
You can see that NVIDIA's these
articles here on Bloomberg.

00:54:29.450 --> 00:54:31.630
NVIDIA's DeepSeek
drop has Wall Street

00:54:31.630 --> 00:54:35.060
fixated with their stock
plunging and stuff like that.

00:54:35.060 --> 00:54:40.510
So it was surprisingly
big splash.

00:54:40.510 --> 00:54:44.890
And then you have a couple
of examples here of the chain

00:54:44.890 --> 00:54:47.620
of thoughts that it does.

00:54:47.620 --> 00:54:49.660
The one on the left
is from their paper.

00:54:49.660 --> 00:54:52.287
It's generating this
answer to this question.

00:54:52.287 --> 00:54:54.620
And then in the middle of it,
like the chain of thought,

00:54:54.620 --> 00:54:56.600
it says, wait, wait,
wait, that's an aha

00:54:56.600 --> 00:54:58.340
moment I can flag here.

00:54:58.340 --> 00:54:59.727
Let's reevaluate this.

00:54:59.727 --> 00:55:01.310
And it starts saying
some other stuff.

00:55:01.310 --> 00:55:07.570
So they were just showing how
it learns to rethink and fix

00:55:07.570 --> 00:55:08.510
its own mistakes.

00:55:08.510 --> 00:55:12.580
And that was all done just
with this training that just

00:55:12.580 --> 00:55:14.240
rewarding the final answer.

00:55:14.240 --> 00:55:15.950
And it was doing
the rest itself.

00:55:15.950 --> 00:55:19.180
And I personally love
the simplicity of it

00:55:19.180 --> 00:55:22.390
that we didn't have to do
really complicated things

00:55:22.390 --> 00:55:23.180
in the middle.

00:55:23.180 --> 00:55:25.540
It's just rewarding the end.

00:55:25.540 --> 00:55:29.410
And you can already get
these impressive results.

00:55:29.410 --> 00:55:33.300
So the one on the right is
kind of a more funny one.

00:55:33.300 --> 00:55:36.520
Ask DeepSeek to select
a random number.

00:55:36.520 --> 00:55:41.670
And it outputs this large
amount of chain of thought

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:44.080
where it overthinks itself.

00:55:44.080 --> 00:55:47.245
Maybe I should go with something
like 7777, but that's four 7s,

00:55:47.245 --> 00:55:48.370
which might be too obvious.

00:55:48.370 --> 00:55:51.450
Alternatively, 1,2,3,
too sequential.

00:55:51.450 --> 00:55:53.790
Whereby if I close
my eyes and 42,

00:55:53.790 --> 00:55:56.860
wait, no, that's from
Hitchhiker's Guide and so on.

00:55:56.860 --> 00:56:01.290
So it's pretty amusing
what it ends up doing.

00:56:01.290 --> 00:56:06.586
But yeah, almost a little
human in a way, though.

00:56:06.586 --> 00:56:08.550
But I don't think
that's actually

00:56:08.550 --> 00:56:10.690
the way we really want to
generate random numbers,

00:56:10.690 --> 00:56:13.860
obviously.

00:56:13.860 --> 00:56:14.550
Yeah.

00:56:14.550 --> 00:56:23.770
So from my own team's work
before that came out so 2024,

00:56:23.770 --> 00:56:30.090
October, we were looking to
extend our chain of thought

00:56:30.090 --> 00:56:32.910
training results from
iterative reasoning preference

00:56:32.910 --> 00:56:33.760
optimization.

00:56:33.760 --> 00:56:36.190
We show that could work quite
well with math problems,

00:56:36.190 --> 00:56:40.650
but we want chain of thought
reasoning for all problems.

00:56:40.650 --> 00:56:43.390
Like even if you're,
I don't know,

00:56:43.390 --> 00:56:48.160
thinking about how
to write a poem,

00:56:48.160 --> 00:56:52.960
you might do a whole bunch of
thinking, write some drafts,

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:55.560
try and understand what
you're going to write about

00:56:55.560 --> 00:57:00.390
before you actually write it,
just to pick an example that's

00:57:00.390 --> 00:57:01.750
very different to math.

00:57:01.750 --> 00:57:06.610
So we try to follow
the same recipe.

00:57:06.610 --> 00:57:10.860
So we have a prompt,
a generic prompt,

00:57:10.860 --> 00:57:13.810
just to initialize the model.

00:57:13.810 --> 00:57:19.210
And we ask it to respond
to whatever generic query,

00:57:19.210 --> 00:57:21.690
but do it in a comprehensive
and detailed way

00:57:21.690 --> 00:57:24.010
by writing your
internal thoughts.

00:57:24.010 --> 00:57:26.610
And then you must include
this draft response

00:57:26.610 --> 00:57:28.090
in its evaluation.

00:57:28.090 --> 00:57:32.800
And after you write your final
response, have this special tag.

00:57:32.800 --> 00:57:36.390
So again, that's so we can
extract the response out

00:57:36.390 --> 00:57:39.990
and separate it
from the thoughts.

00:57:39.990 --> 00:57:42.550
And this is the same
trick that we did before

00:57:42.550 --> 00:57:45.330
and the same trick
that DeepSeek does.

00:57:45.330 --> 00:57:47.920
But here we're going to
do it not on math problems

00:57:47.920 --> 00:57:51.360
but on all kinds of other
instruction following problems.

00:57:51.360 --> 00:57:55.360
But now we won't have a
verifiable reward anymore,

00:57:55.360 --> 00:57:59.100
but we still have these
LLM-as-a-Judge that we were

00:57:59.100 --> 00:58:00.550
using in self-rewarding.

00:58:00.550 --> 00:58:03.240
So we can use that
on the response

00:58:03.240 --> 00:58:06.850
to judge whether the
response is good.

00:58:06.850 --> 00:58:09.990
But the difference between this
and the original self-rewarding

00:58:09.990 --> 00:58:13.650
work is that we have the chain
of thought generation before it.

00:58:13.650 --> 00:58:20.460
So we tried this, and actually,
ended up working quite well.

00:58:20.460 --> 00:58:23.760
It gave gains on AlpacaEval
and another benchmark

00:58:23.760 --> 00:58:24.760
called Arena-Hard.

00:58:24.760 --> 00:58:30.640
It's actually third on the
AlpacaEval leaderboard,

00:58:30.640 --> 00:58:33.960
I think at the time, and it was
the best 8 billion parameter

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:36.000
model on Arena-Hard.

00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:38.680
I think one interesting
thing about the training.

00:58:38.680 --> 00:58:40.590
So again we're doing
this iterative training

00:58:40.590 --> 00:58:42.810
with PPO is that--

00:58:42.810 --> 00:58:49.690
so this is called yeah, thought
preference optimization, TPO.

00:58:49.690 --> 00:58:51.750
And we compare it to
the direct baseline

00:58:51.750 --> 00:58:55.530
in this graph in the bottom
right, which is basically

00:58:55.530 --> 00:58:56.620
no chain of thought.

00:58:56.620 --> 00:59:00.270
And you can see in
the first iterations,

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:04.200
TPO actually makes
the model a lot worse.

00:59:04.200 --> 00:59:06.850
And that's because
these language models--

00:59:06.850 --> 00:59:13.660
so we're using LLAMA
8-bit 8 billion--

00:59:13.660 --> 00:59:16.640
they're already
really well fine tuned

00:59:16.640 --> 00:59:19.350
to be good without
the chain of thought.

00:59:19.350 --> 00:59:23.510
So that had a massive
advantage over trying to make

00:59:23.510 --> 00:59:25.170
it do the chain of thought.

00:59:25.170 --> 00:59:30.290
But after we train it on
successful chain of thought

00:59:30.290 --> 00:59:34.850
outcomes using the
LLM-as-a-Judge or the reward

00:59:34.850 --> 00:59:39.470
model, then you can see
that the gap closes,

00:59:39.470 --> 00:59:42.540
and then it starts improving
on iteration 3 and 4,

00:59:42.540 --> 00:59:44.720
and now it's better than
the direct baseline.

00:59:44.720 --> 00:59:46.770
So yeah.

00:59:46.770 --> 00:59:49.040
So I think this was
really promising

00:59:49.040 --> 00:59:53.610
because it showed you could do
this chain of thought training,

00:59:53.610 --> 00:59:56.527
not just for math problems
or verifiable tasks

00:59:56.527 --> 00:59:57.235
but for anything.

01:00:00.252 --> 01:00:06.590
And yeah, so here's a couple
of examples of things it does.

01:00:06.590 --> 01:00:10.490
So you can say for a
query, write me a poem

01:00:10.490 --> 01:00:13.860
or what dog breed is smallest?

01:00:13.860 --> 01:00:17.630
It's going to do a whole
bunch of thinking, like draft

01:00:17.630 --> 01:00:22.025
responses and evaluation or
various thought processes

01:00:22.025 --> 01:00:24.300
before it gets the output.

01:00:24.300 --> 01:00:24.800
Yeah.

01:00:24.800 --> 01:00:29.810
And then DeepSeek
R1 in their paper,

01:00:29.810 --> 01:00:32.300
not 100% sure if
it's doing something

01:00:32.300 --> 01:00:34.800
the same as what we're
doing there with TPO,

01:00:34.800 --> 01:00:38.660
but there's a hint it
is because it says--

01:00:38.660 --> 01:00:40.890
here I highlighted
it for general data,

01:00:40.890 --> 01:00:43.100
we resort to reward
models to capture

01:00:43.100 --> 01:00:48.680
human preferences in complex
and nuanced scenarios.

01:00:48.680 --> 01:00:51.350
So I think they're also
generating chain of thought

01:00:51.350 --> 01:00:52.290
and then doing that.

01:00:52.290 --> 01:00:56.430
But it wasn't fully
spelled out in their paper,

01:00:56.430 --> 01:00:57.715
so it wasn't completely clear.

01:01:00.770 --> 01:01:04.590
And then there's other
things we can do.

01:01:04.590 --> 01:01:07.260
So these, as far
to my knowledge,

01:01:07.260 --> 01:01:09.540
aren't actually yet in
these bigger models.

01:01:09.540 --> 01:01:12.680
So these are more just
like research papers

01:01:12.680 --> 01:01:17.660
that hopefully then get scaled
up to these bigger models.

01:01:17.660 --> 01:01:24.680
The next work we did to try to
show that the language model so

01:01:24.680 --> 01:01:28.580
far we've shown it's quite good
at improving its instruction

01:01:28.580 --> 01:01:32.780
and reasoning
ability, but we also

01:01:32.780 --> 01:01:38.120
want to explicitly improve its
ability to evaluate itself.

01:01:38.120 --> 01:01:40.970
Because if we can
do that, then when

01:01:40.970 --> 01:01:45.535
it's doing these
reward model judgments,

01:01:45.535 --> 01:01:47.160
it's going to get
better at everything.

01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:49.660
It's going to get better at
structure and following as well.

01:01:49.660 --> 01:01:53.670
So this work is called
Meta-Rewarding language models,

01:01:53.670 --> 01:01:56.870
where it's going to try to
improve its own judgments

01:01:56.870 --> 01:01:59.430
by meta judging its judgments.

01:01:59.430 --> 01:02:03.770
So, obviously, a little
bit meta this work.

01:02:03.770 --> 01:02:08.480
So yeah, now you can see the
language model in three roles.

01:02:08.480 --> 01:02:11.270
As an actor, so
following instructions.

01:02:11.270 --> 01:02:14.990
As a judge, judging responses
to those instructions.

01:02:14.990 --> 01:02:19.440
And as a meta-judge it's going
to judge its own judgments.

01:02:19.440 --> 01:02:22.320
And I'll show you
how you can do that.

01:02:22.320 --> 01:02:24.870
We call that LLM
as a Meta-Judge.

01:02:24.870 --> 01:02:27.270
So there's a prompt for that.

01:02:27.270 --> 01:02:34.310
So you'd have a given
instruction from the user,

01:02:34.310 --> 01:02:39.320
a response from, say, a model,
and then judgment A and judgment

01:02:39.320 --> 01:02:41.330
B, which could include
the chain of thought

01:02:41.330 --> 01:02:43.460
and the final verdict.

01:02:43.460 --> 01:02:46.280
And then you want
the language model

01:02:46.280 --> 01:02:49.160
to judge which of the
judgments is better,

01:02:49.160 --> 01:02:53.480
A or B. Now, we have a
particular algorithm then

01:02:53.480 --> 01:02:57.590
for basically taking
all different judgments,

01:02:57.590 --> 01:02:59.990
calculating the meta
judgments, and then the Elo

01:02:59.990 --> 01:03:01.500
score amongst them.

01:03:01.500 --> 01:03:06.410
But the reason to do that is
to create preference pairs

01:03:06.410 --> 01:03:08.400
amongst judgments.

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:10.480
So now we can say,
OK, this judgment

01:03:10.480 --> 01:03:14.920
looks better than that judgment
because our meta-judge tend

01:03:14.920 --> 01:03:15.920
to say that.

01:03:15.920 --> 01:03:20.650
And so we can construct
preference pairs again

01:03:20.650 --> 01:03:24.410
with DPO of which
judgments are better.

01:03:24.410 --> 01:03:26.570
So now we've got two
types of preference pairs,

01:03:26.570 --> 01:03:30.340
the ones we had before
which were like response.

01:03:30.340 --> 01:03:33.560
Response chosen is better
than response rejected.

01:03:33.560 --> 01:03:37.420
But now we also
have judgment chosen

01:03:37.420 --> 01:03:39.380
is better than
judgment rejected.

01:03:39.380 --> 01:03:43.070
And we train the model
with both types of data.

01:03:43.070 --> 01:03:44.500
So this is different
to basically

01:03:44.500 --> 01:03:48.340
self-rewarding language models
that I showed earlier only

01:03:48.340 --> 01:03:51.110
had the top part
of this picture.

01:03:51.110 --> 01:03:54.100
So it didn't really have
an explicit mechanism

01:03:54.100 --> 01:03:58.090
to improve its ability
to evaluate things.

01:03:58.090 --> 01:04:05.920
And yeah, so this work, I think,
it's exciting because it helps.

01:04:05.920 --> 01:04:15.260
We actually get better win rates
on say AlpacaEval with that.

01:04:15.260 --> 01:04:23.740
And yeah, this plot
you can see again we

01:04:23.740 --> 01:04:26.150
see the improvement
over iterations.

01:04:26.150 --> 01:04:31.450
But it's above the performance
of the self-rewarding model that

01:04:31.450 --> 01:04:34.420
doesn't have this
meta-judge training.

01:04:34.420 --> 01:04:38.380
And even the
self-rewarding model

01:04:38.380 --> 01:04:41.390
tends to plateau a bit
here on iteration 4,

01:04:41.390 --> 01:04:46.960
whereas it's still going up
for the meta rewarding model.

01:04:46.960 --> 01:04:48.920
So I think that's
pretty exciting.

01:04:48.920 --> 01:04:52.510
And yeah, I've
got one more thing

01:04:52.510 --> 01:04:59.710
to talk about, which is how to
improve the chain of thoughts

01:04:59.710 --> 01:05:03.770
for judgments for evaluation.

01:05:03.770 --> 01:05:08.890
So that's another recent work
actually only out last week.

01:05:08.890 --> 01:05:15.400
So this work is called
Thinking LLM-as-a-Judge.

01:05:15.400 --> 01:05:21.730
And the goal is to generate
these long chain of thoughts

01:05:21.730 --> 01:05:26.590
similar to R1 but for the
task of judgment of responses.

01:05:26.590 --> 01:05:29.720
Again, so that we have good
rewards for training our models.

01:05:29.720 --> 01:05:31.990
So here's an example
from the model

01:05:31.990 --> 01:05:35.320
we trained of what it can do.

01:05:35.320 --> 01:05:38.950
Outputs this evaluation plan
of how it's going to evaluate,

01:05:38.950 --> 01:05:43.780
and then executes on the plan
and gives this final verdict.

01:05:43.780 --> 01:05:44.350
Yeah.

01:05:44.350 --> 01:05:48.880
So we have a training
mechanism for that.

01:05:48.880 --> 01:05:59.530
And the trick here is you can
look at the evaluation task

01:05:59.530 --> 01:06:05.830
actually as a verifiable task if
you know which of two responses

01:06:05.830 --> 01:06:06.410
is better.

01:06:06.410 --> 01:06:08.035
So let's say you've
got an instruction.

01:06:08.035 --> 01:06:09.860
You've got response
A and response B.

01:06:09.860 --> 01:06:13.310
If you know in advance that
A is better or B is better,

01:06:13.310 --> 01:06:16.880
then that's like a verifiable
task like the math tasks.

01:06:16.880 --> 01:06:20.080
We can compute the
reward directly.

01:06:20.080 --> 01:06:24.190
We don't need to say a
language model LLM is a judge

01:06:24.190 --> 01:06:29.030
to agree with our output.

01:06:29.030 --> 01:06:32.860
So the trick in this
work, the first trick

01:06:32.860 --> 01:06:37.750
is to generate synthetic
evaluation data

01:06:37.750 --> 01:06:40.240
so that we have a
verifiable task so we

01:06:40.240 --> 01:06:41.710
know which one's better.

01:06:41.710 --> 01:06:44.210
And once we have
that verifiable task,

01:06:44.210 --> 01:06:46.450
we can essentially
do something a bit

01:06:46.450 --> 01:06:50.350
like the iterative reasoning
preference optimization, IRPO,

01:06:50.350 --> 01:06:53.920
because we can compute the
rewards directly and build

01:06:53.920 --> 01:06:58.600
these preference pairs to
improve the chain of thoughts

01:06:58.600 --> 01:07:01.840
for evaluation.

01:07:01.840 --> 01:07:03.480
There's a couple
of extra details

01:07:03.480 --> 01:07:05.740
here where in this
particular case,

01:07:05.740 --> 01:07:09.870
we actually asked it to
do a plan first and then

01:07:09.870 --> 01:07:14.940
execute on the plan, which
is this 2 is the left hand

01:07:14.940 --> 01:07:16.265
part of this figure.

01:07:18.990 --> 01:07:23.950
So how do we create
this verifiable data?

01:07:23.950 --> 01:07:26.220
So this comes from
a previous paper

01:07:26.220 --> 01:07:29.880
that we did called
self-taught evaluators.

01:07:29.880 --> 01:07:35.040
What we're going to do
is given a prompt x,

01:07:35.040 --> 01:07:38.190
we generate a good response
with a language model

01:07:38.190 --> 01:07:40.150
or at least we think
is a good response.

01:07:40.150 --> 01:07:42.100
Let's say it's a
reasonable response.

01:07:42.100 --> 01:07:47.650
And then we generate a
similar prompt x prime,

01:07:47.650 --> 01:07:50.350
which is similar
but different to x.

01:07:50.350 --> 01:07:54.780
And then we also use same LLM
to generate a good response

01:07:54.780 --> 01:07:55.840
to x prime.

01:07:55.840 --> 01:07:57.420
We'll call that y prime.

01:07:57.420 --> 01:08:01.540
And now the hope
is that, of course,

01:08:01.540 --> 01:08:04.470
because x prime
is different to x,

01:08:04.470 --> 01:08:09.790
y prime won't be as
good a response to x.

01:08:09.790 --> 01:08:10.330
Right?

01:08:10.330 --> 01:08:15.510
So that's the thing that
basically y should be preferred

01:08:15.510 --> 01:08:18.010
over y prime for input x.

01:08:18.010 --> 01:08:20.819
So this now becomes
our verifiable task

01:08:20.819 --> 01:08:22.779
that we're going to
use for training.

01:08:22.779 --> 01:08:27.479
So the only question there
that was a bit of an unknown

01:08:27.479 --> 01:08:30.819
was how do you make this
similar but different prompts?

01:08:30.819 --> 01:08:33.069
And, of course, we use an
LLM to do that as well.

01:08:33.069 --> 01:08:36.689
So we say please generate a
modified instruction that's

01:08:36.689 --> 01:08:38.460
highly relevant but
not semantically

01:08:38.460 --> 01:08:40.330
identical to the
instruction above.

01:08:40.330 --> 01:08:44.520
And then this is what gives us
the similar instruction which

01:08:44.520 --> 01:08:47.970
we can then generate
the lower quality

01:08:47.970 --> 01:08:52.590
response for the original
instruction from.

01:08:52.590 --> 01:08:58.710
And yeah, that's roughly the
high level picture of this work.

01:08:58.710 --> 01:09:01.380
There's some
ablation studies here

01:09:01.380 --> 01:09:05.250
where we showed
that these plans are

01:09:05.250 --> 01:09:10.470
superior to not thinking at
all, so no chain of thought.

01:09:10.470 --> 01:09:14.819
But in general, it's good to
have the plans as unconstrained

01:09:14.819 --> 01:09:15.633
as possible.

01:09:15.633 --> 01:09:17.050
Like, if you
encourage them to be,

01:09:17.050 --> 01:09:21.689
for example, lists of criteria
or verification questions

01:09:21.689 --> 01:09:25.510
which are some other works
have used with prompting,

01:09:25.510 --> 01:09:27.790
then the results get worse.

01:09:27.790 --> 01:09:32.640
And I think that's in line
with the O1, R1 style results

01:09:32.640 --> 01:09:34.350
as well, where
basically you don't

01:09:34.350 --> 01:09:37.649
want to constrain too much
the chain of thought thinking

01:09:37.649 --> 01:09:39.310
processes of these
language models,

01:09:39.310 --> 01:09:43.050
but you want to
train and find out

01:09:43.050 --> 01:09:47.640
what it's going to be good at.

01:09:47.640 --> 01:09:52.479
And then, yeah, after we train
this model using LLAMA-3.1

01:09:52.479 --> 01:09:58.630
or 3.3-70B as a base where
it gets a very good results.

01:09:58.630 --> 01:10:02.250
Actually, state of the art
on this benchmark called

01:10:02.250 --> 01:10:07.350
RewardBench, which evaluates
reward models for LLM-as-a-Judge

01:10:07.350 --> 01:10:09.030
generative models.

01:10:09.030 --> 01:10:12.900
And it's also quite good
on some harder evaluation

01:10:12.900 --> 01:10:17.250
tasks on some newer benchmarks
that just came out like RM-Bench

01:10:17.250 --> 01:10:19.070
and FollowBench Eval.

01:10:22.170 --> 01:10:27.587
And yeah, that's really all the
things I'm going to talk about.

01:10:27.587 --> 01:10:29.170
I think I talked
about enough already.

01:10:29.170 --> 01:10:33.990
So I'm just a little bit of a
summary of these recent works

01:10:33.990 --> 01:10:36.900
from the last year.

01:10:36.900 --> 01:10:40.793
Self-rewarding
models they're models

01:10:40.793 --> 01:10:42.460
that can train
themselves to get better.

01:10:42.460 --> 01:10:46.050
And the hope is that could
be a path to superhuman AI

01:10:46.050 --> 01:10:49.200
as the model improves itself.

01:10:49.200 --> 01:10:51.900
We've seen how
verifiable rewards

01:10:51.900 --> 01:10:55.240
can help to train chain of
thought for better reasoning.

01:10:55.240 --> 01:10:57.770
So I showed you this
iterative reasoning preference

01:10:57.770 --> 01:10:59.820
optimization technique.

01:10:59.820 --> 01:11:05.900
But DeepSeek R1 have related
techniques basically all using

01:11:05.900 --> 01:11:08.420
these verifiable
rewards on the output,

01:11:08.420 --> 01:11:11.690
and then letting
the chain of thought

01:11:11.690 --> 01:11:16.110
don't penalize it, just
find ones that work well

01:11:16.110 --> 01:11:19.730
during your training cycle.

01:11:19.730 --> 01:11:22.700
And then you can also use
these verifiable rewards

01:11:22.700 --> 01:11:25.230
to improve the evaluation
ability of the model.

01:11:25.230 --> 01:11:28.490
So that's the thinking
LLM-as-a-judge that I just

01:11:28.490 --> 01:11:29.450
showed.

01:11:29.450 --> 01:11:32.600
And then, of course, if we
have these better judges

01:11:32.600 --> 01:11:38.280
with these chain of thought, the
whole thing can go in a cycle.

01:11:38.280 --> 01:11:42.930
We can use those to help train
to think on nonverifiable tasks,

01:11:42.930 --> 01:11:47.750
because we can use this Thinking
LLM-as-a-Judge to train those

01:11:47.750 --> 01:11:48.270
models.

01:11:48.270 --> 01:11:52.070
And we call that thinking LLMs,
which I showed earlier as well.

01:11:52.070 --> 01:11:57.290
And then, finally, you can use
this meta-rewarding concept

01:11:57.290 --> 01:12:00.470
to judge their own judgments
is another way of improving

01:12:00.470 --> 01:12:02.340
their evaluation ability.

01:12:02.340 --> 01:12:06.590
So I've shown you a
few separate papers.

01:12:06.590 --> 01:12:11.090
But the goal of the
bigger model release

01:12:11.090 --> 01:12:13.890
is to try and put some
of these ideas together.

01:12:13.890 --> 01:12:15.950
So yeah, some of those
more recent things

01:12:15.950 --> 01:12:19.250
like meta-rewarding
and thinking LLMs

01:12:19.250 --> 01:12:25.070
need to go together with the
earlier ideas all in one system.

01:12:25.070 --> 01:12:28.110
And we'll see how
far we can push that.

01:12:28.110 --> 01:12:30.930
And, of course, still
looking for new ideas.

01:12:30.930 --> 01:12:35.430
And they're going to be coming
in the coming days, weeks,

01:12:35.430 --> 01:12:37.260
months, and years, I'm sure.

01:12:37.260 --> 01:12:45.260
So yeah, of course, there's
lots of other directions.

01:12:45.260 --> 01:12:49.880
And just to throw one out
there is all the reasoning

01:12:49.880 --> 01:12:54.620
that I've been talking
about was with chain

01:12:54.620 --> 01:12:56.820
of thoughts that were text.

01:12:56.820 --> 01:12:59.250
It's generating text
from the language model.

01:12:59.250 --> 01:13:01.650
Now, you don't have
to do that right.

01:13:01.650 --> 01:13:05.540
You could the transformer
itself does reasoning

01:13:05.540 --> 01:13:11.610
by all these multiplications
inside the neural net.

01:13:11.610 --> 01:13:14.720
It's all manipulating
vectors in the hidden

01:13:14.720 --> 01:13:16.080
states of the network.

01:13:16.080 --> 01:13:21.080
And so why does this system 2
reasoning have to be tokens?

01:13:21.080 --> 01:13:25.350
Maybe that could also be this
continuous vectors as well.

01:13:25.350 --> 01:13:29.300
So we explored that in
a recent paper method

01:13:29.300 --> 01:13:34.160
called COCONUT, where we
replace the chain of thought

01:13:34.160 --> 01:13:38.610
of outputting tokens with
outputting vectors instead.

01:13:38.610 --> 01:13:43.220
And we showed that has
some promise, actually.

01:13:43.220 --> 01:13:48.380
It could at least match the
performance on some tasks

01:13:48.380 --> 01:13:52.820
and even outperform classical
chain of thought on some more

01:13:52.820 --> 01:13:55.400
difficult search tasks.

01:13:55.400 --> 01:13:58.230
But these are all quite
small scale tasks,

01:13:58.230 --> 01:14:03.120
so it remains to be seen how far
people can push that direction.

01:14:03.120 --> 01:14:05.450
I think there's
some reasons to want

01:14:05.450 --> 01:14:09.330
to have the chain of
thought in human language,

01:14:09.330 --> 01:14:10.440
natural language.

01:14:10.440 --> 01:14:16.820
I think it's a nice thing for
safety and interpretability.

01:14:16.820 --> 01:14:22.010
But, as I said, the
neural network itself

01:14:22.010 --> 01:14:24.320
isn't interpretable in
that way, the internals

01:14:24.320 --> 01:14:25.260
of the transformer.

01:14:25.260 --> 01:14:30.650
So there's something we
have to work out, I think.

01:14:30.650 --> 01:14:34.680
And I got another slide
about what else comes next.

01:14:34.680 --> 01:14:37.760
I think there's so much more
exciting research to be done.

01:14:37.760 --> 01:14:42.890
So Ilya Sutskever
in his "Seq2Seq"

01:14:42.890 --> 01:14:49.540
paper Test of Time Award that
was this last NeurIPS talk he

01:14:49.540 --> 01:14:52.360
presented some things.

01:14:52.360 --> 01:14:54.500
This is a picture of
what he was saying.

01:14:54.500 --> 01:14:55.220
What comes next?

01:14:55.220 --> 01:14:59.000
He was mentioning agents,
synthetic data, inference time

01:14:59.000 --> 01:15:02.150
compute, reasoning,
understanding,

01:15:02.150 --> 01:15:05.050
and being self-aware,
which all makes sense.

01:15:05.050 --> 01:15:07.300
I think these are all
things that people

01:15:07.300 --> 01:15:09.410
are working on today.

01:15:09.410 --> 01:15:13.550
Well, maybe the self-aware
one is a little more fuzzy,

01:15:13.550 --> 01:15:15.500
but the other ones at least.

01:15:15.500 --> 01:15:19.970
And yeah, I would just add
some more detail there.

01:15:19.970 --> 01:15:25.030
I think self-improving,
self-evaluation

01:15:25.030 --> 01:15:28.990
is really important because
that bottlenecks performance.

01:15:28.990 --> 01:15:33.100
So using more of this inference
time compute for evaluation

01:15:33.100 --> 01:15:35.493
is only going to help
self improve the model.

01:15:35.493 --> 01:15:36.910
And I think that's
sort of related

01:15:36.910 --> 01:15:40.840
to this self-aware
comment that he

01:15:40.840 --> 01:15:44.560
makes here that a
self-aware model might

01:15:44.560 --> 01:15:47.770
be more able to
understand what it knows

01:15:47.770 --> 01:15:51.340
and what it doesn't know,
which is evaluation.

01:15:51.340 --> 01:15:54.080
And then learning
from interaction.

01:15:54.080 --> 01:15:56.740
I think learning how to
reason by actually interacting

01:15:56.740 --> 01:16:00.130
with people in the world,
internet, or itself,

01:16:00.130 --> 01:16:01.460
are important things.

01:16:01.460 --> 01:16:04.150
And so that's related
to the agents point

01:16:04.150 --> 01:16:07.060
and the synthetic data
point he makes there.

01:16:07.060 --> 01:16:09.280
And then, of course,
it's not just

01:16:09.280 --> 01:16:11.750
about improving the
system 2 reasoning.

01:16:11.750 --> 01:16:15.130
I think if we can improve
system 1 reasoning,

01:16:15.130 --> 01:16:17.320
so like the transformer
architecture

01:16:17.320 --> 01:16:20.030
itself, then that's great too.

01:16:20.030 --> 01:16:25.330
So maybe there's a better
attention model, some sort of--

01:16:25.330 --> 01:16:27.145
or maybe something new.

01:16:27.145 --> 01:16:29.830
A neural network layer
we haven't come up

01:16:29.830 --> 01:16:36.820
with yet that could change the
scaling laws and the performance

01:16:36.820 --> 01:16:37.580
of these models.

01:16:37.580 --> 01:16:38.570
And that's great too.

01:16:38.570 --> 01:16:41.770
So yeah, plenty to do.

01:16:41.770 --> 01:16:45.670
And it's going to be
exciting to see what happens.

01:16:45.670 --> 01:16:47.700
Thank you.

